Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Offers. <omitted> (b) In evaluating any Acquisition Proposal, the Company Board shall, in good faith and after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisors of nationally recognized reputation, determine whether such Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Company Board's determination shall be based on, among other factors, the financial terms of the proposal, the strategic fit of the proposal with the Company’s long-term goals, the likelihood of the proposal being consummated, and the potential legal ramifications of accepting such proposal. Furthermore, the Company Board shall consider the impact of the proposal on the Company’s stakeholders, including employees, customers, and suppliers. If the Company Board determines that the proposal is a Superior Proposal, it may proceed with a Company Adverse Recommendation Change, provided that it has complied with Section 9.4 and given Parent written notice of its intent to make such a change, allowing Parent three Business Days to respond with a counter-proposal. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
