Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Evaluation of Offers. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, at any time during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may reconsider an Acquisition Proposal if, and only if, (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and independent auditors, that the proposal complies with all applicable Laws and regulations, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to reconsider the Acquisition Proposal not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fourth Business Day after Parent receives such written notice, (iii) the decision to reconsider the Acquisition Proposal is based on a comprehensive review that includes strategic alignment, legal compliance, and potential synergies, and (iv) the Company Board, after consultation with a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, determines in good faith that the financial terms of the Acquisition Proposal are favorable, but also considers the overall strategic benefit to the Company. The Company must comply with clauses (1) through (6), as follows: (1) the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal based on financial and strategic criteria, (2) the Company shall have made available to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal, (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent to allow Parent to amend the terms of this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer be superior, (4) after evaluating the results of negotiations with Parent, the Company Board shall have determined that the strategic and legal benefits of the Acquisition Proposal remain superior, (5) the Company shall have complied with all fiduciary duties under applicable Law, and (6) if the Company intends to proceed with the Acquisition Proposal, it shall have complied with Section 10.1(a)(i). For clarity, this Section 8.3(f) shall also apply to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
