Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. Board Evaluation. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding any provision in this Agreement to the contrary, during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may proceed with a Board Recommendation Change only if (i) the Company Board determines in good faith, after receiving advice from the Company\u2019s external legal counsel and financial advisor, that not making such a change would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with a written notice of the Company Board\u2019s intention to proceed with a Board Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement in favor of a Superior Proposal not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fourth Business Day after Parent receives this written notice, and (iii) the decision to proceed with a Board Recommendation Change is related to an Acquisition Proposal or the intention to terminate this Agreement in favor of a Superior Proposal, and the Company has complied with the following clauses: (1) before implementing clauses (2) through (5), the Company Board must have determined that the Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company must have provided Parent with both oral and written details of the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and all related communications, (3) the Company must have engaged in good faith negotiations with Parent to allow Parent to adjust this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer be deemed a Superior Proposal, (4) after considering the outcomes of negotiations with Parent and the proposals made by Parent, if any, after consultation with its external legal counsel and financial advisor, the Company Board must have determined in good faith that the Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal and that failing to proceed with a Board Recommendation Change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, and (5) if the Company intends to terminate this Agreement in favor of a Superior Proposal, it must comply with Section 10.2(a). Furthermore, the provisions of this Section 9.4(f) apply to any amendments to the financial terms or other material amendments to any Acquisition Proposal, with any reference to four Business Days being adjusted to two Business Days. (Pages 85-87)\nAnswer:
